<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ariège (department)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1790">1790</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ariège is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>, created in 1790 from the County of Foix and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/couserans">Couserans</a>, with its prefecture at Foix. Named after the river Ariège, it is mountainous and thinly populated, and its castles such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montsegur">Montségur</a> are associated with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catharism">Cathars</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ariege-department.jpg" alt="Ariège (department)" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Olybrius · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mont%C3%A9gut-en-Couserans_-_Ussau_-_20110702_(1).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ariège, named after the river Ariège, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> in the Occitanie region of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and its capital is Foix. It covers 4,890 square kilometres and is divided into three arrondissements, Foix, Pamiers and Saint-Girons. To the south it borders Andorra and the Spanish province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lleida">Lleida</a>. The north is plain, hills and low valleys given to agriculture; below the mountains lie the Pyrenean foothills; and the high country is dominated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>, with the Pica d'Estats at 3,143 m the highest point of the department. Its only true glacier is that of Mont Valier. Its inhabitants are called Ariègeois, and in 2023 there were 155,722 of them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Ariège is one of the original 83 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> created during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> on 4 March 1790. It was formed from the counties of Foix and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/couserans">Couserans</a>. A request to rename it Ariège-Pyrénées, to promote it better across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, was rejected. Foix has a fortress, the Château de Foix, on a hill above the town; it was attacked many times without being captured, including by Simon de Montfort, and was later used as a prison. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montsegur">Montségur</a> stands on a rocky outcrop at 1,200 metres. During the Albigensian Crusade and the siege of 1244 the castle was largely destroyed, and more than two hundred Cathar priests were burnt at the stake as heretics.</p><p>In the early 17th century the Huguenot rebellions ravaged the area. In 1621 Huguenot forces ruined the church at La Tour-du-Crieu, and in 1629 Pamiers was sacked by Henry of Condé after uprisings that left several hundred dead. The abbeys at Foix, Tarascon-sur-Ariège, Saint-Girons, Saverdun and Le Mas-d'Azil were burned in the same period.</p><p>The 19th century brought strong industrial growth, supported by abundant water power and reserves of iron ore. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> plant was established at Pamiers in 1817, and forges and blast furnaces followed at Montgaillard and Tarascon-sur-Ariège. Changes in forest laws in 1829 led to the War of the Maidens, a revolt of peasants who disguised themselves as women while resisting the loss of their rights to use the forests.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Ariège is known for its rural and mountainous landscape; more than half of it is mountainous and 490,965 hectares are forest. The Regional Natural Park of the Ariège Pyrenees, created in 2009, covers about 40 per cent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a>. Talc is mined in the Ax valleys, and the winter sports resorts include Ax 3 Domaines and Guzet-Neige. The patriotic song Arièjo O moun Pais, written by Father Sabas Maury, born in 1863, serves as its anthem.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ariège (department)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Known from Pliny and later Roman sources, the Consoranni lived in the central Pyrenees in the modern <b>Ariège</b>, between the Convenae and the Volcae Tectosages.</blockquote><a href="/state/consoranni">Consoranni</a></li></ul></section>
